[echo] {{answer}}:
Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** recipe that shows how to colour a `circle` layer in MapLibre GL JS according to a numeric `population` property – the larger the population, the darker the colour.

It covers:

| What you need | Why it matters |
|---------------|----------------|
| **A vector (or GeoJSON) source** that contains a numeric `population` field on each feature | The expression reads that field with `["get","population"]`. |
| **Correct `source-layer` name** (for vector sources) | If the name is wrong the layer draws nothing – no error is thrown. |
| **A colour ramp** (e.g. light-yellow → dark-red) | `["interpolate", …]` maps the numeric value to a colour. |
| **Optional radius scaling** | Often you want the circles to grow with population as well. |
| **CORS / glyphs / sprite** (if you also have labels) | Not needed for the colour itself, but a blank map is usually a CORS problem. |

---

## 1️⃣ Minimal working example

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Population-coloured circles</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},          // we’ll add sources later
      layers: []            // we’ll add layers later
    },
    center: [-98.5, 39.8], // USA centre
    zoom: 4
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Add a source – replace with your own URL / data
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // Example using a public GeoJSON of US cities (replace with yours)
  map.addSource('cities', {
    type: 'geojson',
    // If you already have a vector tile source, use `type: 'vector'` and `url`/`tiles`
    data: 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/plotly/datasets/master/geojson-counties-fips.json',
    // If the population field lives inside `properties.population`,
    // you can promote it to the feature id (optional, not needed for colour):
    // promoteId: 'population'   // only if you need feature-state later
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Add the circle layer
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'city-circles',
    type: 'circle',
    source: 'cities',
    // source-layer: 'my_layer'   // <-- ONLY for vector sources
    paint: {
      // ---- 3a️⃣ Circle radius (optional, also data-driven) ----
      // Here we make the radius grow with population, but you can skip it.
      'circle-radius': [
        'interpolate',
        ['linear'],
        ['get', 'population'],
        0,      2,      // 0 people → 2 px
        1000,   4,
        10000,  8,
        100000, 12,
        1000000, 20   // 1 M → 20 px
      ],

      // ---- 3b️⃣ Circle colour (the core of the answer) ----
      // Larger population → darker colour.
      // We use a yellow-to-red ramp, but you can pick any colours.
      'circle-color': [
        'interpolate',
        ['linear'],               // linear interpolation between stops
        ['get', 'population'],    // the numeric property
        0,    '#ffffb2',          // light yellow for 0
        1000, '#fecc5c',
        10000, '#fd8d3c',
        50000, '#f03b20',
        100000, '#bd0026'         // dark red for 100 k+
      ],

      // ---- 3c️⃣ Optional visual tweaks ----
      'circle-opacity': 0.7,
      'circle-stroke-width': 1,
      'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
    }
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣ (Optional) Add a label layer so you can see the numbers
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'city-labels',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'cities',
    layout: {
      'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'population']],
      'text-size': 12,
      'text-offset': [0, 1.5]
    },
    paint: {
      'text-color': '#000',
      'text-halo-color': '#fff',
      'text-halo-width': 1
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the key bits do

| Code snippet | Explanation |
|--------------|-------------|
| `'circle-color': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['get','population'], …]` | **Data-driven styling** – MapLibre reads the `population` property for each feature and linearly interpolates between the colour stops you provide. |
| `0, '#ffffb2', 1000, '#fecc5c', …` | The **stops**. The first number is the population value, the second is the colour (hex, rgb, hsl, etc.). Anything between two stops gets a colour that is a blend of the two. |
| `'circle-radius': ['interpolate', …]` | Same technique, but for size. You can omit this if you only care about colour. |
| `circle-opacity`, `circle-stroke-*` | Cosmetic tweaks that make the circles easier to see on top of a basemap. |

---

## 2️⃣ How to adapt this to **your own data**

### a) Vector tiles (instead of GeoJSON)

If you are using a vector tile source (e.g. a TileJSON endpoint), the only differences are:

```js
map.addSource('mytiles', {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'https://example.com/tiles.json'   // <-- TileJSON endpoint
  // OR
  // tiles: ['https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'],
  // minzoom: 0,
  // maxzoom: 14,
  // tileSize: 512   // default for vector tiles
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-circles',
  type: 'circle',
  source: 'mytiles',
  'source-layer': 'places',   // <-- **exact** name from the TileJSON's vector_layers
  paint: { … same paint block as above … }
});
```

*Tip:* Open the TileJSON (`https://example.com/tiles.json`) in a browser or `curl`. Look for the `vector_layers` array – it tells you the exact `source-layer` names and the fields each layer contains. If the `vector_layers` field is missing, you can usually find the name in the provider’s docs (e.g. `transportation`, `water`, `places`).

### b) Property is a **string** instead of a number

If `population` comes in as a string (e.g. `"12345"`), coerce it to a number:

```js
'circle-color': [
  'interpolate',
  ['linear'],
  ['to-number', ['get', 'population']],
  0, '#ffffb2',
  1000, '#fecc5c',
  …
]
```

### c) Want a **step** (discrete bins) instead of a smooth gradient

```js
'circle-color': [
  'step',
  ['get', 'population'],
  '#ffffb2',   // default colour for values < 1000
  1000, '#fecc5c',
  10000, '#fd8d3c',
  50000, '#f03b20',
  100000, '#bd0026'
]
```

`step` is useful when you want clear “low / medium / high” categories.

### d) Using a **different colour palette**

You can generate a palette with any colour-tool (e.g. https://colorbrewer2.org/, https://d3js.org/). Paste the hex values into the stops. For a **sequential** (light → dark) palette you might use:

```js
'circle-color': [
  'interpolate',
  ['linear'],
  ['get', 'population'],
  0,   '#f7fbff',
  10,  '#deebf7',
  100, '#c6dbef',
  1000, '#9ecae1',
  10000, '#6baed6',
  100000, '#3182bd',
  1000000, '#08519c'
]
```

