Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** pattern that will colour a `circle` layer darker as the numeric `population` property gets larger.  
It works for both **GeoJSON** and **vector-tile** sources – the only difference is the `source-layer` name you have to supply for a vector source.

---

## 1️⃣  Make sure the source is wired correctly

| What you need | Why it matters for the colour expression |
|---------------|------------------------------------------|
| **Source type** (`geojson` or `vector`) | Determines whether you need a `source-layer` name. |
| **`source-layer`** (vector only) | Must match the exact layer name that appears in the TileJSON `vector_layers` list. A typo → the circles never draw, but no error is shown. |
| **`population` property** | Must be a **numeric** field (integer or float) on every feature. If it is a string, `["to-number", ["get", "population"]]` can coerce it. |
| **CORS / glyphs / sprite** | Not needed for a pure circle layer, but make sure the tile server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` if it’s on another domain. |

```js
// Example: a GeoJSON source
map.addSource('cities', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: 'https://example.com/data/cities.geojson'   // or a JS object
});

// Example: a vector source (TileJSON endpoint preferred)
map.addSource('cities-vt', {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'https://example.com/tiles/cities.json'      // TileJSON that contains the tiles array
  // If you only have a template, use `tiles: [...]` and add minzoom/maxzoom yourself
});
```

> **Quick sanity-check** – Open the TileJSON (or the GeoJSON file) in the browser and look for the exact `source-layer` name. For the OpenMapTiles schema it would be something like `"places"` or `"settlements"`; for a custom tileset you’ll see it under `vector_layers[].id`.

---

## 2️⃣  Add the circle layer with a data-driven colour expression

```js
// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Choose the source you added above
// ---------------------------------------------------
const sourceId   = 'cities';      // or 'cities-vt'
const sourceLayer = 'places';    // <-- ONLY for vector sources! Omit for GeoJSON

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Define colour stops – larger pop → darker colour
// ---------------------------------------------------
// You can pick any colour ramp you like. Below we use a simple
// light-to-dark blue. Feel free to replace the hex values with
// your own palette (e.g. #fee5d9 → #a50f15 for a red ramp).
const colourStops = [
  // [population value, colour]
  [  0,   '#e0f3ff' ],   // very small towns – light blue
  [ 10_000, '#c6e2ff' ],
  [ 50_000, '#8abed8' ],
  [100_000, '#5699c1' ],
  [500_000, '#2c6ea5' ],
  [1_000_000, '#004080' ] // megacities – darkest blue
];

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Add the layer
// ---------------------------------------------------
map.addLayer({
  id: 'city-circles',
  type: 'circle',
  source: sourceId,
  // ONLY for vector sources:
  ...(sourceLayer && { 'source-layer': sourceLayer }),

  // OPTIONAL: make the circles larger for larger cities
  // (you can keep this static if you only care about colour)
  'paint': {
    // ---- radius (optional) ----
    // Here we also interpolate radius so big cities look bigger.
    // Remove this block if you want a fixed radius.
    'circle-radius': [
      'interpolate',
      ['linear'],
      ['get', 'population'],
      0,          4,   // tiny towns → 4 px
      10_000,    6,
      100_000,  10,
      1_000_000, 16   // megacity → 16 px
    ],

    // ---- colour (the core of the answer) ----
    'circle-color': [
      'interpolate',
      ['linear'],
      // If the property might be a string, coerce it:
      // ['to-number', ['get', 'population']]
      ['get', 'population'],
      ...colourStops.flat()   // expands to: 0, '#e0f3ff', 10000, '#c6e2ff', …
    ],

    // ---- a little visual polish (optional) ----
    'circle-stroke-width': 1,
    'circle-stroke-color': '#fff',
    'circle-opacity': 0.8
  }
});
```

### How the expression works

* `['interpolate', ['linear'], ['get', 'population'], …]` tells MapLibre to **linearly interpolate** between the colour stops you provide.
* The first numeric argument after `['get', 'population']` is the **lower bound** (e.g. `0` → `#e0f3ff`).  
  The next pair is the next bound (`10 000` → `#c6e2ff`), and so on.
* For any value **between** two stops, MapLibre blends the colours proportionally, giving a smooth darkening effect.
* If a feature’s `population` is **outside** the range you defined, the colour of the nearest stop is used (i.e. values > 1 000 000 stay `#004080`).

---

## 3️⃣  Common pitfalls & quick-diagnosis checklist

| Symptom | What to look at |
|---------|-----------------|
| **No circles at all** | • Verify the source loads (check Network tab for 200s). <br>• For vector sources, double-check the `source-layer` name against the TileJSON `vector_layers`. <br>• Ensure the `population` property exists on the features (`console.log(feature.properties)` in a `data` event). |
| **Circles appear but are all the same colour** | • The `population` field may be a **string**; use `['to-number', ['get', 'population']]` in the expression. <br>• The property may be missing on many features → fallback to `null` → expression returns the first stop. |
| **Colours look wrong (e.g., light where they should be dark)** | • Check the order of the stops – the **largest value should map to the darkest colour**. <br>• Make sure you didn’t accidentally reverse the colour array. |
| **Map is blank but network shows 200s** | • CORS headers missing on the tile server → browser blocks the response. <br>• For raster tiles, verify `tileSize: 256` if you’re using classic OSM tiles (default is 512). |
| **Hover/selection state doesn’t work** | • Feature IDs are required. If you need `setFeatureState`, add `promoteId: 'population'` (or another unique property) to the source definition. |

